From 12954a089e92328d27a7e4bf478539edbcede90a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 03:40:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] player: Player.Process(video.sidedata), the original Dolby
 Vision and HDR payloads of the picture on screen

Add-ons that parse the Dolby Vision RPU or the HDR metadata of what is playing (TinyPPI
and the like) read them from this label on the CoreELEC builds. It is now here, with the
same name and the same JSON: dovi.config, dovi.rpu (HEVC: the escaped NAL unit 62 with
its 7C 01 header, as the stream coded it; AV1: the ITU-T T.35 payload), hdr10plus, mdcv,
cll, flags and structure, each payload base64 encoded. The format is Team CoreELEC's
(read from its xbmc branch aml-5.15.196-22.0, commit
6da7084a843bf3e39a46b8b13a91eb4d9f2ce20a, AMLFrameMetadata.h and CEGUIInfoLabels.dox, as
documentation of the format); the code is written here. Thanks to CoreELEC for the label.

Nothing is collected until something reads the label. A read starts the collection for ten
seconds; the decoder then makes an immutable snapshot of each picture (FFmpeg's
DOVI_RPU_BUFFER is the NAL unit without its two header bytes, so the header goes in front;
the HDR10+ payload is FFmpeg's own serialization with the T.35 header in front), the render
manager hands the snapshot of the picture it chooses to be shown to a store with a lock of
its own, and the JSON is built when the label is read, from that snapshot, holding nothing
of the player or the renderer. While nobody reads, each picture costs one relaxed load in
the decoder and one in the render manager, and a null shared_ptr copied into the queue slot.

flags says "converted" where Hades rebuilds a Dolby Vision video as HDR10 or SDR or makes
Dolby Vision of HDR10+ or of the picture. The enhancement layer of a dual layer stream is
st-dl in the same track and dt-dl in a second one.

The format is in utils/SideData.h, with no Kodi code in it so that a test runs on the host.
